  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nToday will be the last day of no precipitation to be expected\nacross the area. Slightly warmer than average temperatures can\nbe expected, with light winds. An Alberta clipper-like shortwave\nwill interact with another shortwave in the late morning/early\nafternoon over the Great Lakes, which will lead to the formation\nof a more organized system. As it approaches the area, it will\nbring widespread rain showers to the area starting overnight\ntonight, and lasting through the day tomorrow as it moves over \nthe area. Since temperatures will remain above freezing \novernight tonight and into tomorrow, precipitation will remain \nas rain. Though, this won't be the case heading into tomorrow \nnight as temperatures begin to drop, which will be discussed in\nKey Message 2.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAs the main aforementioned low pressure system moves west to\neast across the area tomorrow, we start to see temperatures drop\ntomorrow night from northerly flow from Canada. As the system\ncontinues to bring precipitation across the area, we'll start to\nsee precipitation types vary as temperatures approach freezing,\nand dip below freezing. Going into Friday, temperatures only\nclimb above freezing at peak heating times, but will drop back\nbelow freezing later that evening. Through tomorrow night and\nFriday, any snow accumulation will be up to an inch in higher\nelevation areas, and up to a few tenths of an inch for most\nother areas. However, with the ground remaining quite warm, it's\npossible for most places to see any frozen precipitation melt\npretty soon after hitting the ground, especially in the daytime\nhours. For higher elevations, it may stick a little longer, but\nwith high temperatures on Friday being above freezing almost\neverywhere, snow accumulation isn't likely to stick well.\n\nThe center broad trough moves south and over the Great Lakes on\nFriday and a portion of Saturday, which may elongate the time of\nlighter precipitation, especially for Saturday.\n\nHigh pressure builds in late this weekend into early next week \nwith a gradual moderation of temperatures while remaining mostly\ndry.",
